WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.769
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here, and today we’re
unpacking the truly singular persona of Bill

00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:13.879
Murray. Since replacing Chevy Chase on SNL
in 1977 Bill Murray has embodied a very particular

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.580
kind of comedy that can best be described
as “ironic and cooly distant.”

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.640
"I don't worry about anything."

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.320
Over the years, Murray has curiously transcended the label of “that goofy dude in movies” to

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.340
essentially that of a comedy legend.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:26.980
"Who's Bill Murray?"

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.080
"I mean that's like asking who Ghandi is."

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.280
Last year the New York Times ran a piece exploring

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.040
Murray’s “Peculiar Ascent” from the
status of comedian to the level of a “secular saint.”

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.100
"I'm a god."

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.840
Apparently, the amount of pop culture
paraphernalia dedicated to Murray rivals that

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.039
of James Dean, Marilyn Monroe, and Albert
Einstein.

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:48.859
There’s a truly particular irony to Murray’s
existence. He has a cameo in Parks and Rec

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:53.300
where he plays a corpse, which is ONLY funny
BECAUSE it’s Bill Murray. The entire plot

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.900
of Osmosis Jones takes place inside Bill Murray’s
body, which seems like the producers telling us:

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.640
Hey, isn't this funny how they're inside Bill  Murray?

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.700
And for some reason, he has a cameo in Space
Jam where he plays… himself.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.220
"Producer's a friend of mine. Just had a teamster come and drop me off."

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.340
So… what’s the deal with this guy? Is
this just cult of personality run amok? Or

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.360
is there something more going on here? Welcome
to this Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.810
of Bill Murray. Have you ever noticed how,
in many Bill Murray movies, he doesn’t seem

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.700
to be taking the plot seriously?

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.020
"Real wrath of God type stuff: fire and brimstone coming down from the skies."

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.240
"Human sacrifice, dogs and cats living together, mass hysteria!"

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.060
He stands slightly at a distance from everything, enabling

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.100
him to maintain a dryly humorous commentary
about what’s going on around him.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.900
"What the hell kinda clown are you?"

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.080
"The crying-on-the-inside kind I guess."

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:45.380
"Why worry? Each of us is wearing an unlicensed nuclear accelerator on his back."

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.840
Murray is perpetually sarcastic, even during otherwise
dramatic moments, so much so that he often

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:57.280
verges on or outright breaks the reality of
the scene. Murray just doesn’t give a fuck.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.409
It’s kinda his thing.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:03.380
It’s as if his very presence is license
to deflate the stakes of an otherwise dramatic moment.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.320
"Congratulations, you're a father."

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.200
When his ship is boarded by bloodthirsty
pirates in The Life Aquatic, Murray inexplicably

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.860
goes Rambo and takes back the boat despite
the fact his character is not a badass. At

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.800
all. The entire premise of The Man Who Knew
Too Little is essentially Murray playing a

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.350
man who has no personal stake in the plot
surrounding him, because he literally believes

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.860
it is the world's most convincing game of
improv.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.580
"Relax, we're actors."

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.760
This kind of detatched comedic irony isn’t
new nor is it unique to Murray. For one thing,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.970
it’s a staple of American comedy dating
back at least to vaudeville days. In Marx

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.550
Brothers movies, for instance, the stories
were merely there to provide some sort of

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.840
skeletal structure for the jokes, and Groucho
would frequently break the fourth wall.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.520
"Pardon me while I have a strange interlude. This would be a better world for children if the parents had to eat the spinach."

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:57.010
In fact, this ironic, detatched kind of comedy
goes back to 500 years before the Marx Brothers.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.260
Japanese playwright and philosopher Zeami Motokiyo

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.660
trained actors to conceal their intentions
as they acted, even from themselves. Much

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.330
later German poet/playwrite Bertolt Brecht, having been
influenced by Japanese theater, trained his

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.810
actors to develop a style where everything
on stage was acted out in quotation marks,

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.840
so that every line is divorced from it’s
intended dramatic weight. He wanted to make

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.810
sure that neither his actors nor the audience
fully identified with the characters on stage.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.900
The purpose of this was to inspire the audience
to break free of conventional thinking and

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.940
bring them a new experience- to make them
think again.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.720
Over the last few years Bill Murray has been
making headlines for crashing parties, photobombing,

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:39.230
and randomly popping up in people's lives.
In June of 2014 he turned up at the pre-wedding

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.060
photoshoot for a couple he’d never even
met, In 2011 he turned up at Karaoke One 7

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:49.760
in NYC and joined the party of some rando.
He served as a Bartender at SXSW 2010 for

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.650
no apparent reason. And, perhaps best of all,
in 2009 he turned up at the construction site

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.650
for New York City’s new Poets House to read
a poem and dole out some advice.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.030
For a big celebrity, he's also notoriously hard to get a hold of.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.550
He doesn't have an agent and forces producers to contact him through a 1-800 hotline

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.340
he checks only a few times a year.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.160
Just what is Murray up to?

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.800
"What the hell are you doing?"

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.540
"I have no idea."

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.460
Why does he spend
his time doing stunts like this instead of

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.630
filling yachts with models and absconding
to Eurpoean villas like other celebrities?

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.260
One explanation might be Murray’s relationship
to Sufi mystic GI Gurdjieff. According to

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:29.680
the late Harold Ramis, Murray was a student
of Gurdjieff, a kind of guru who “used to

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.350
act really irrationally to his students almost
as if trying to teach them object lessons.”

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.660
Gurdjieff, like most mystics, aimed at freeing
the minds of men from illusion, or waking

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.540
people up to the reality of their own freedom.
He was a teacher of something he called the

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.850
fourth way to enlightenment or, alternatively,
the way of the sly man. The sly man doesn’t

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.570
have to hide himself away in a monastery or
practice abstinence or asceticism, but can

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.760
find the truth in everyday life.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.370
The first step in Gurdjieff’s method is
to divide your attention. You have to become

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.400
aware of both the external world and your
own inner state and then you have to refuse

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.790
to get caught up in either world. The purpose
of this separation is to take control over

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:12.100
your own behavior and reactions. The sly man
creates distance between the world, the self,

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.940
and the self that is observing everything.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.340
"It doesn't matter! It just doesn't matter!"

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.760
What makes Murray unique is that he turns
the usual American style of comedic irony

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.100
against itself, or against himself. The result
is a bit mystifying.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.500
Irony traditionally means that the character’s
words or actions are clear to the audience

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:35.930
but unknown to the character. Murray takes
this a step further. He gives us ironic performances

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:41.050
wherein his characters are unaware of the
meaning of their own activity, but we know

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:45.400
that Bill Murray knows. In Ghostbusters, for
instance, when the woman he loves transforms

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.580
in to a demon, his reaction is not what one
would expect.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.940
"Okay, so, she's a dog."

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.620
The fact that it seems outlandish

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:58.560
for his character to react with such nonchalance
is exactly the point. The audience identifies

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:02.940
not with Bill Murray’s character, but with
Bill Murray, who distances himself from the

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.100
stakes of the narrative.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:09.360
In 1993’s Groundhog Day this ironic distance
and self-awareness becomes his whole character

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.340
arc. Through each repetition Murray’s character
becomes more aware. First of his

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:18.480
surroundings, then of other people until he's finally able to achieve some distance from himself and his

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.080
selfishness and can accept love and find some peace in the small town of Punxsutawney.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.820
"Anything different is good."

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.490
So what do you think: Is 
a sarcastic approach to daily life the way to go?

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.860
"So do you have any regrets?"

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:30.800
"Garfield, maybe."

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.840
Is Bill Murray's whole career an intervention, an attempt to wake people up to some kind of enlightenment?

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.520
"I like to be more consistently here, you know, really in it, really alive."

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.560
Or is it all a joke? Let us know what you think in the comments.

